    /// <remarks>
    ///   <para>&lt;http://purl.oclc.org/NET/ssnx/qu/dim#Duration&gt;</para>
    ///   <para>The wave directional spectrum can be written as a  five dimensional function S(t,x,y,f,theta) where t is time, x and y are horizontal coordinates (such as longitude and latitude), f is frequency and theta is direction.  S has the standard name sea_surface_wave_directional_variance_spectral_density.  S can be integrated over direction to give S1= integral(S dtheta) and this quantity has the standard name sea_surface_wave_variance_spectral_density.  Frequency moments, M(n) of S1 can then be calculated as follows: M(n) = integral(S1 f^n df), where f^n is f to the power of n.  The first wave period, T(m1)  is calculated as the ratio M(0)/M(1).</para>
    /// labels<para>sea surface wave mean period from variance spectral density first frequency moment</para></remarks>
    /// <seealso href="http://purl.oclc.org/NET/ssnx/cf/cf-property#sea_surface_wave_mean_period_from_variance_spectral_density_first_frequency_moment">http://purl.oclc.org/NET/ssnx/cf/cf-property#sea_surface_wave_mean_period_from_variance_spectral_density_first_frequency_moment</seealso>
